    "created_at": "2021-12-13T17:36:54Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-12-13T17:36:54Z",
    "body": "I think @dandclark is on the right track. The main thing to consider is how well such ideas fit with possible future extensions.\r\n\r\n- As @bmeck says, what happens if we add future import assertions to the web? This seems quite unlikely, and so maybe not worth complicating the spec for. But we should consciously decide one way or another.\r\n- Relatedly, https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/7367 brings up a way to make inline JSON and CSS modules useful. Although there are more complications there, as I outline in https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/7415 , maybe the syntax we use for modulepreload could help with that design, or at least match with it?\r\n\r\nFor example, as a strawperson, you could imagine something like `assert=\"... JSON string ...\"` and then the syntax would be something like\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<link rel=\"modulepreload\" href=\"style.css\" assert='{ type: \"css\" }'>\r\n\r\n<script type=\"module\" assert='{ type: \"css\" }'>\r\n.class { prop: value; }\r\n</script>\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis unfortunately has the usual quote-related problems of putting JSON in attributes, so it's mostly just a strawperson. But it's worth thinking about.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
